l-to-include-each-query"]},{"id":"graphs","name":"Graphs: BFS and DFS","topics":["Graph Theory","Breadth-First Search","Depth-First Search"],"why":"Islands, grids and networks. BFS gives shortest steps, DFS explores regions. Always keep a visited set.","problems":["flood-fill","find-if-path-exists-in-graph","number-of-islands","max-area-of-island","island-perimeter","keys-and-rooms","number-of-provinces","clone-graph","rotting-oranges","01-matrix","surrounded-regions","number-of-enclaves","pacific-atlantic-water-flow","is-graph-bipartite","shortest-path-in-binary-matrix","open-the-lock","snakes-and-ladders","word-ladder"]},{"id":"graphs-advanced","name":"Graphs: advanced","topics":["Topological Sort","Union-Find","Shortest Path","Minimum Spanning Tree"],"why":"Topological sort, union-find, Dijkstra and MST. Google loves graphs, so being solid here puts you ahead of most candidates.","problems":["course-schedule","course-schedule-ii","find-eventual-safe-states","redundant-connection","number-of-operations-to-make-network-connected","accounts-merge","most-stones-removed-with-same-row-or-column","evaluate-division","network-delay-time","path-with-minimum-effort","cheapest-flights-within-k-stops","number-of-ways-to-arrive-at-destination","find-the-city-with-the-smallest-number-of-neighbors-at-a-threshold-distance","min-cost-to-connect-all-points","swim-in-rising-water","making-a-large-island","reconstruct-itinerary","critical-connections-in-a-network","word-ladder-ii"],"bridge":[{"id":"ac:practice2_a","why":"Disjoint Set Union. Write union-find with path compression and union by size, then check it here."},{"id":"ac:practice2_g","why":"SCC. Strongly connected components, printed in topological order."}]},{"id":"dp-1d","name":"Dynamic programming: 1D","topics":["Dynamic Programming","Memoization"],"why":"Start with recursion, add memoization, then turn it into a table. Go slowly: DP clicks after 20–30 problems.","problems":["climbing-stairs","min-cost-climbing-stairs","house-robber","house-robber-ii","delete-and-earn","decode-ways","coin-change","perfect-squares","maximum-product-subarray","word-break","combination-sum-iv","longest-increasing-subsequence","partition-equal-subset-sum"],"bridge":[{"id":"ac:dp_a","why":"Frog 1. Cheapest way to reach stone i from i-1 or i-2: the same shape as Min Cost Climbing Stairs, with contest-style input."},{"id":"ac:dp_b","why":"Frog 2. Frog 1 with up to K jumps back. Teaches you to read the constraints: O(N·K) is fine here."},{"id":"ac:dp_c","why":"Vacation. dp[day][activity]: add a small second dimension when the choice today depends on yesterday."}]},{"id":"dp-2d","name":"Dynamic programming: 2D and strings","topics":["Dynamic Programming","Knapsack"],"why":"Grids, two strings and knapsack. Most of Google's DP questions are variations of these.","problems":["unique-paths","unique-paths-ii","minimum-path-sum","triangle","minimum-falling-path-sum","maximal-square","longest-common-subsequence","longest-palindromic-subsequence","palindromic-substrings","minimum-insertion-steps-to-make-a-string-palindrome","edit-distance","coin-change-ii","target-sum","interleaving-string","distinct-subsequences","shortest-common-supersequence","cherry-pickup-ii","wildcard-matching","regular-expression-matching"],"bridge":[{"id":"ac:dp_d","why":"Knapsack 1. The textbook 0/1 knapsack, indexed by weight."},{"id":"ac:dp_e","why":"Knapsack 2. Same problem, but the weight limit is huge: flip the state to index by value instead."},{"id":"ac:dp_f","why":"LCS. Longest common subsequence, and you must print the string itself, not just its length."},{"id":"ac:dp_h","why":"Grid 1. Count paths in a grid with walls, modulo 10^9+7."}]},{"id":"dp-advanced","name":"Dynamic programming: advanced","topics":["Dynamic Programming","Bitmask"],"why":"Stock problems, interval DP, LIS variants, DP on trees and bitmask DP. This is where hard interview questions live.","problems":["best-time-to-buy-and-sell-stock-with-cooldown","best-time-t