 trees, string matching, tricky design questions and hard classics. This is what separates a Google offer from a near miss.","milestone":"You've covered everything Google's coding rounds test. Finish with the final step below.","problems":["range-sum-query-mutable","count-of-smaller-numbers-after-self","reverse-pairs","count-of-range-sum","the-skyline-problem","sliding-window-median","shortest-palindrome","repeated-string-match","longest-duplicate-substring","max-points-on-a-line","snapshot-array","stock-price-fluctuation","lfu-cache","all-oone-data-structure"],"bridge":[{"id":"ac:practice2_b","why":"Fenwick Tree. Point update and range sum in O(log n): the simplest range-query structure."},{"id":"ac:practice2_j","why":"Segment Tree. Point update, range max, and binary search on the tree."}]},{"id":"google-final","name":"Final step: Google's most asked (last 1 year)","topics":["How to Approach a Problem"],"why":"Real questions Google asked in the last year that the earlier steps didn't cover, most frequent first. Solve them like a mock interview: 45 minutes each, talk out loud, no hints for the first 30 minutes.","milestone":"Path complete. Keep your revisions going and do mock interviews: you're ready for Google's coding rounds.","problems":["number-of-visible-people-in-a-queue","first-missing-positive","swap-adjacent-in-lr-string","longest-valid-parentheses","text-justification","reverse-linked-list-ii","insert-delete-getrandom-o1","range-module","guess-the-word","random-pick-with-weight","frequency-of-the-most-frequent-element","find-all-duplicates-in-an-array","exclusive-time-of-functions","rearrange-array-elements-by-sign","separate-squares-i","longest-non-decreasing-subarray-after-replacing-at-most-one-element","smallest-integer-divisible-by-k","longest-balanced-subarray-ii","champagne-tower","minimum-number-of-increments-on-subarrays-to-form-a-target-array","maximum-product-of-splitted-binary-tree","predict-the-winner","smallest-subsequence-of-distinct-charac